#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe737141-ad09-5074-a083-76726e85f623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348685} {:answer/id #uuid "33ead3e0-fc70-56f6-8afb-71efb4a4d2d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348686} {:answer/id #uuid "db620620-db04-5fb8-9c14-a41a1a4eda6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348687} {:answer/id #uuid "56fc8f88-a739-5fc3-b5f9-3bf1645d1e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348688} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3acfa2-1e34-5082-8bfc-e96bebf4eb4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33ead3e0-fc70-56f6-8afb-71efb4a4d2d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348686} :answer-field/id #uuid "6947c113-d031-508a-b35f-6d00aa5fbb2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "45d81332-88c1-5b53-8983-3fa0c83fde53" :question/math-academy-id "q-105695" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064672/assets/3cc62b0a51519230bd1230874c00e17f165c345d11bc64e6d3c8fcf7788a5ee8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is not irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064672/assets/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into from the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddf23edb-1c7e-5a7a-a63f-18ad36e72c44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348692} {:answer/id #uuid "3e53b892-e209-5b0a-b009-a87684ba7a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348693} {:answer/id #uuid "a92aeb6b-7acc-5a15-b47e-ca5c67da5706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+2{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348694} {:answer/id #uuid "e284c632-b7ce-58b4-9355-7b884ffe9af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348695} {:answer/id #uuid "f15473f2-f7f7-5be0-ad13-9030cf119470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f15473f2-f7f7-5be0-ad13-9030cf119470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348696} :answer-field/id #uuid "372af865-2473-5848-8737-a2f5490bd34d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "56768610-071f-5826-8038-3b23e4d39690" :question/math-academy-id "q-131344" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y)=({y}^{2}+{e}^{x})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j,$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2y \\\\  & =y(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b65b190-b587-5827-a160-b8df85b6227c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Scalar Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa529fc2-f6f0-5885-a71e-3f511a4aad6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348699} {:answer/id #uuid "065cedf5-9bab-593c-984c-3ce8c5e1b3e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348700} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab7f801-0d22-5057-9260-3f04b859cf34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348701} {:answer/id #uuid "a86d0f20-45be-5a83-b04c-12f29d22abd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348702} {:answer/id #uuid "47f8816b-53dc-54d6-9e6f-521e42be3795" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a86d0f20-45be-5a83-b04c-12f29d22abd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348702} :answer-field/id #uuid "a20ed83d-6dbb-5df3-8d63-7e1abce79b7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a0fbb73-17a7-5477-a476-848bd5a49fd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-131348" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=({x}^{2}+xy)\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\,j.$ Evaluate the scalar curl of $F$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y))-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+y}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the scalar curl at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0),$ and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{(1/2,0)} & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2}+0)}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5759cd00-873a-5339-9098-3f3c870f3f7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348706} {:answer/id #uuid "96290768-da07-5255-901f-28473f135ada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348707} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc277bc-0b07-522a-bad0-6bf3464db126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348708} {:answer/id #uuid "e2495c6f-dfd1-5308-85aa-74ada0f29969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348709} {:answer/id #uuid "9861f9e0-ee22-5715-8db2-36be0666daac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96290768-da07-5255-901f-28473f135ada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348707} :answer-field/id #uuid "74dedc58-2d4d-5b56-bbd7-6b41ec1aa502" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ecf0697a-f0a9-5859-b0bb-dbd418346e6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-131361" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064672/assets/1709a94b7361a1e8764d94199f9e84802ae925a09e6481250d0846b83bb6549f.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction.\n- $F$ is irrotational" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064672/assets/7c831425eae7afd709d546a8e05100f495adc04bba6450cfa56d370b415a28f5.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate clockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into from the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d00be64f-9377-58f2-917e-277ae8b51964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348713} {:answer/id #uuid "40409e0c-ce6b-5902-89f2-d788d06bdb0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348714} {:answer/id #uuid "12f29151-c153-53ae-b0cb-076873d7290b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348715} {:answer/id #uuid "2731bfdb-1a51-551d-ac05-8f841eb76754" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348716} {:answer/id #uuid "35f4ff4f-de33-5e72-ba90-669aaa29622c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d00be64f-9377-58f2-917e-277ae8b51964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348713} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e7dcdce-e166-5f3b-b003-53db65f3faeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2dcaf3bb-a51b-51ee-bef2-7f7484d8f502" :question/math-academy-id "q-131366" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064672/assets/781a4e33b8b55762b2dcb7575d08315b5842f83d6d78a5136a1c353eefd3069d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the negative $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14064672/assets/979b63f21e56924bd7880f9487cf829089e38cf14d287e70196c7a661e41f97f.png)\n\nMoreover, if $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, whereas statement II is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would not rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ the vector field $F$ is irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F=0,$ it points in neither the positive nor the negative $z$-direction.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
